      <h2 class="text-xl sm:text-2xl font-bold text-white mt-10 mb-4">
        2. Production Pitfalls & Hardening Rules
      </h2>
      <ul class="list-disc list-inside space-y-2 text-xs text-slate-300">
        <li><strong>Lock Starvation on Process Death:</strong> Always configure an explicit TTL (e.g. <code>ex=30</code>) on the distributed lock. If a worker process is OOM-killed by the kernel mid-execution, the lock expires automatically, allowing the payment gateway's next retry to proceed.</li>
        <li><strong>Token Validation on Unlock:</strong> Never use a bare <code>redis.delete(lock_key)</code>. If processing takes 32 seconds and the lock expired after 30 seconds, a bare delete would destroy the lock acquired by a second worker. Always use a cryptographically unique UUID token and verify ownership via Lua.</li>
        <li><strong>Database Constraint as Final Defense:</strong> Distributed Redis caching provides fast, non-blocking pre-filtering. However, always enforce a <code>UNIQUE CONSTRAINT (gateway_event_id)</code> in your relational PostgreSQL database as the ultimate immutable backstop.</li>
      </ul>
